Byron A Jeff wrote:
> Dealing in absolutes isn't always a good thing. Protection makes sense
> for everyone.

Was that supposed to be ironic?

> In short you unbalance the game in for favor for a while to obtain the
> reward of unbalancing the game in your favor permanently with the
> protection AC. And unlike praying for protection, it's guaranteed to be
> doable in the early game.

No, it's not guaranteed.  Minetown may be a bones file with no priest,
for one; for another, your pet may get squished by a rolling boulder
trap, or fall through a trap door, neither of which is preventable 100%
of the time.  (Levelled up pets *should* be able to survive a rolling
boulder, but if they just came off of a battle, they may not; and pets
only avoid traps that you've found--if you're following the pet, as is
generally the case in protection runs, your pet will find the trapdoor
first.)  Similarly, you or your pet may find a teleportation trap,
forcing you to fight across the level to find Fido, gaining more levels
than you'd planned.  And, of course, the mines levels leading to the
town may be a bones, perhaps the infamous "gnome with a wand of death",
or a large nasty (feral Archon named Spot, for instance) that prevents
you from getting to minetown (unless you're able and willing to dig
through the floor to get there, potentially trapping yourself in the
lower mines with no good way to return to the dungeon).  And we're
completely ignoring the people who try petless.

My current Valk hasn't seen a priest yet, and she's been everywhere
short of the Valley.  Yes, she'll be able to buy some protection there,
but at level 15--hardly a protection racket.

I typically start off by attempting the MPR, but periodically,
something will come up which makes it much harder/impossible to
complete the run, at which point I change strategies as needed.  Which
is the real trick of the MPR--you need to be able to recognize when to
call it off, and figure out what to do to afterwards.  The MPR is a
valid strategy, but it's not one that every character will be able to
pull off, just as running Gehennom from the bottom up is valid (and
useful!), but not always feasible.

